<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">East Francia</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">East <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francia">Francia</a> was the eastern kingdom created when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carolingian-empire">Carolingian Empire</a> was divided by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-verdun">Treaty of Verdun</a> in 843, initially under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-the-german">Louis the German</a>. Covering much of present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, it developed into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-germany">Kingdom of Germany</a> and became the core of the later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/east-francia.jpg" alt="East Francia" width="320" height="243" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: maix¿?; derivative work by Alphathon · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:East_Francia_843.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>In August 843, after three years of civil war following the death of Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-the-pious">Louis the Pious</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-verdun">Treaty of Verdun</a> was signed by his three sons. The division was largely based on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meuse">Meuse</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scheldt">Scheldt</a>, Saone and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhone">Rhone</a> rivers. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lothair-i">Lothair I</a> kept the imperial title and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-francia">Middle Francia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-the-bald">Charles the Bald</a> received <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-francia">West Francia</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-the-german">Louis the German</a> received the eastern, mostly Germanic-speaking lands: the Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austrasia">Austrasia</a>, Alamannia, the Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a> and the March of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carinthia">Carinthia</a>. Regino of Prüm wrote that its different peoples could be told apart by race, customs, language and laws.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In 869 Lotharingia was divided between West and East Francia by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-meerssen">Treaty of Meerssen</a>. In 888 nobles deposed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-the-fat">Charles the Fat</a>, who had briefly reunited the Frankish lands, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arnulf-of-carinthia">Arnulf of Carinthia</a> was elected king in the East. As royal power weakened, the dukes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swabia">Swabia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franconia">Franconia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a> and Lotharingia became hereditary rulers of their territories.</p><p>On 10 November 911 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxon</a>, Franconian, Bavarian and Swabian nobles, no longer electing a Carolingian, chose one of their own, Conrad I. He struggled to establish authority over the other dukes, and on his deathbed named Henry of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a> as his successor. Henry subdued the dukes and built a state apparatus that his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otto-i">Otto I</a> used fully. After Otto was crowned emperor in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> in 962, the era of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a> began.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Because <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-the-german">Louis the German</a> received none of the Carolingian regalia, the symbols and rituals of East Frankish kingship were created anew. The kingdom had a more formal notion of royal election than <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-francia">West Francia</a>, and a coronation liturgy of about 900 has the people asked whether they will obey the king and answer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fiat">Fiat</a>, fiat. By the 12th century Otto of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/freising">Freising</a> had to explain that the eastern kingdom of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a> was now called the kingdom of the Germans. The division, reinforced by the language split between Germanic and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> speech, gradually hardened into separate kingdoms: East <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francia">Francia</a> became the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-germany">Kingdom of Germany</a> and West Francia the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “East Francia” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Treaty of Verdun (843 CE) formalised a threefold division into West Francia under Charles the Bald, Middle Francia (from the Low Countries through Burgundy to Italy) under Lothair, and <b>East Francia</b> under Louis the German.</blockquote><a href="/empire/carolingian-empire">Carolingian Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>In the early 10th century, amid Magyar raids, the Luitpolding Arnulf made it a stem duchy within <b>East Francia</b>, and it remained one of the great duchies of the Holy Roman Empire.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-bavaria">Duchy of Bavaria · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Historians debate when the Liudolfings became dukes in a formal sense; their power grew from the defence of <b>East Francia</b> against Vikings, Slavs and Magyars.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-saxony">Duchy of Saxony · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Frankish priests served <b>East Francia</b>'s interests, so in 861 Rastislav asked Pope Nicholas I for missionaries who knew Slavic and, receiving no answer, turned to Emperor Michael III, who sent Constantine and Methodius in 863.</blockquote><a href="/empire/great-moravia">Great Moravia · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>The Treaty of Verdun in August 843 divided the realm into three kingdoms: West Francia under Charles, <b>East Francia</b> under Louis, and Middle Francia, running from the North Sea to Italy, under Lothar, who kept the imperial title.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-the-franks">Kingdom of the Franks · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The Duchy of Saxony, ruled by the Liudolfing family, produced the kings of <b>East Francia</b> from 919, beginning with Henry the Fowler, and his son Otto I was crowned emperor in 962.</blockquote><a href="/empire/saxons">Saxons · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
